1
00:00:02,293 --> 00:00:05,707
Theme 05: Interruptible Real-Time Agents.

2
00:00:08,491 --> 00:00:12,032
Assistants take turns: listen, think, speak. People don't.

3
00:00:12,624 --> 00:00:17,723
They cut in, correct themselves and change their mind while a search or a booking is still running.

4
00:00:18,315 --> 00:00:24,267
Our agent keeps talking, cancels the work nobody wants any more, re-plans, and never books twice.

5
00:00:25,432 --> 00:00:28,291
Events come in on one queue, actions go out on another.

6
00:00:28,684 --> 00:00:35,361
Only protocol.py knows the evaluation kit's field names, so the real kit is a one-file change.

7
00:00:35,754 --> 00:00:41,429
The fast path is rules only, no LLM, so the first answer goes out on the same event tick.

8
00:00:41,822 --> 00:00:48,200
The coordinator owns every tool call: cancel tokens, generation counters and an idempotency ledger.

9
00:00:48,593 --> 00:00:52,881
Speech recognition and OCR run as cancellable background tasks.

10
00:00:53,274 --> 00:00:59,525
A watchdog closes any turn still open at 110 seconds, before the 120-second cap.

11
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:06,535
Scenario 1: the user changes the destination mid-search.

12
00:01:09,604 --> 00:01:12,868
“Search flights from Bangalore to Delhi on October 5th.”

13
00:01:15,938 --> 00:01:20,717
At 0.4 s the agent says what it is doing and starts the Delhi search.

14
00:01:23,787 --> 00:01:29,120
At 1.9 s the user interrupts: “Actually, make that Hyderabad instead.”

15
00:01:32,190 --> 00:01:36,691
On that same tick the Delhi search is cancelled, before the agent says anything.

16
00:01:39,761 --> 00:01:44,710
A Hyderabad search starts, and the snapshot now says destination = Hyderabad.

17
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:54,500
At 4.9 s the result lands: two flights. Anything the Delhi call returns later is ignored.

18
00:02:02,447 --> 00:02:04,943
Scenario 2: the duplicate-booking trap.

19
00:02:08,569 --> 00:02:13,134
“Book flight AI-202 for Rahul Verma.” The booking takes six seconds.

20
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:24,589
At 2.5 s: “Did that go through? Please book AI-202.” The agent says it is still working and sends nothing.

21
00:02:28,214 --> 00:02:34,764
At 4.5 s: “Just book it now please.” Still nothing sent: the ledger holds the booking's key.

22
00:02:38,389 --> 00:02:44,277
At 6.0 s: one booking, reference RV4T9N. Not three.

23
00:02:52,676 --> 00:02:56,580
Scenario 3: a camera frame and “How do I fix the error on this?”

24
00:03:01,481 --> 00:03:07,134
The frame is ambiguous, so the agent doesn't guess. It asks: “Which device is this about?”

25
00:03:12,035 --> 00:03:15,534
“The washer. It's showing 4C.” A second frame arrives.

26
00:03:20,435 --> 00:03:27,773
The agent looks up the manual for model WW90T and answers from it: error 4C, water supply.

27
00:03:36,129 --> 00:03:42,188
We can't see the hidden set, so we built our own: 60 adversarial scenarios in 13 families.

28
00:03:43,997 --> 00:03:51,037
30 text, 18 audio, 12 visual, scored by a replica of the guide's rubric. Suite score: 100.

29
00:03:52,846 --> 00:04:00,270
100 again on a fresh seed it was never tuned on. First spoken action: 0.69 ms median.

30
00:04:02,078 --> 00:04:07,412
These are our own simulator and scorer. The official evaluation kit isn't released yet.

31
00:04:10,555 --> 00:04:15,312
Not done yet: the kit's field names are still guesses, isolated in one file.

32
00:04:16,282 --> 00:04:20,826
Whisper and OCR are built and tested, but not switched on by default.

33
00:04:23,077 --> 00:04:28,923
Next: conform to the real kit, then an on-device intent model and streaming speech. Thank you.

